WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 14 gave the internal resolution ladder and said that going outside the organisation is not appropriate except where legally prescribed — which is why section 18.1's internal route has to work. Chapter 15 refused the facilitation payment and named the laws without describing them. Chapter 17 built the climate in which somebody is willing to speak. This chapter completes all three.
18.1  A whistleblowing framework
Whistleblowing is the raising of a concern about wrongdoing by somebody inside the organisation, through a route that does not depend on the goodwill of the person the concern is about. A whistleblowing framework is the mechanism that makes it possible, and the outcome asks why it matters to an ethical culture.
Five reasons, and they build on each other.
It is how wrongdoing is found. More occupational fraud is detected by tips than by internal audit, external audit and management review combined, and most of those tips come from employees. No control detects what somebody already knows.
It makes the code operative. Chapter 16 observed that a code with standards and no route is half a code: the framework is the route, and without it an employee who reads the code has been told what is expected and not how to act on it.
It gives the resolution ladder of Chapter 14 somewhere to go when the ladder itself is blocked — when the supervisor is implicated, or when two levels of management are.
It provides early warning. A concern raised when something is irregular is cheap; the same matter after a loss, a regulator or a journalist is not.
And its existence changes behaviour before it is ever used. Somebody contemplating an act behaves differently where any of several colleagues could report it without being identified.
What makes a framework work, which is the examinable half.
Several routes, not one, including at least one outside the management line — the audit committee chair, an independent hotline, an external provider.
Confidentiality, and anonymity as an option. Anonymity makes investigation harder and some people will use nothing else; a framework that offers only named reporting excludes exactly the concerns that carry most risk to the reporter.
Protection against retaliation, stated explicitly, enforced visibly, and extended to dismissal, demotion, exclusion, reassignment and the quieter forms — the person who is suddenly not invited to meetings. Retaliation is often unlawful as well, and in several jurisdictions carries its own penalties independent of the original wrongdoing.
Accessibility: available to everybody including suppliers, agents and contractors, in the local language, outside working hours, and usable by people without company email.
A defined process: acknowledgement, triage, investigation by somebody independent of the subject, a decision, and — as far as confidentiality permits — feedback to the person who raised it. Nothing kills a framework faster than concerns that disappear.
Records and reporting: the number of concerns, their categories, how long they took and what happened, reported to the audit committee. Chapter 16 proposed exactly this in place of training completion rates.
And a demonstrated history. The only thing that persuades the second person to report is what happened to the first.
Two difficulties an honest answer acknowledges. Not every report is well founded, and a few are malicious; the framework must therefore protect the subject of a report as well as its author, through confidentiality and a proper investigation, and a person who reports in good faith must be protected even when the concern proves unfounded. And a framework in a company with the wrong tone does not work: the employee's calculation is about what they expect to happen, which is set by Chapter 17 rather than by the policy.
