18.2  Anti-bribery laws
The outcome asks for the purpose of anti-bribery laws and names two. Both reach conduct far outside their own countries, which is why they matter to a company like Orontes.
The purpose, stated generally, is threefold: to prevent the corruption of public officials and of commercial decisions; to protect fair competition, since a market in which contracts are won by payment rather than by merit allocates resources badly and raises costs for everybody; and to protect the integrity of financial records, since a bribe must always be disguised in the books as something else.
The US Foreign Corrupt Practices Act has two parts, and candidates are expected to know both.
The anti-bribery provisions prohibit offering, paying, promising or authorising anything of value to a foreign official, directly or through an intermediary, in order to obtain or retain business or to secure an improper advantage. Intermediaries are expressly covered, which is why Chapter 15's agent is the central exposure.
The accounting provisions require issuers to keep books and records that accurately and fairly reflect transactions, and to maintain a system of internal accounting control. These are enforced far more often than the bribery provisions themselves, because a mis-described payment is easier to prove than the corrupt intent behind it — and they are the provisions that make this a management accountant's subject directly.
Two exceptions and defences are examinable. There is a narrow exception for facilitating payments to expedite routine governmental action, which is drawn tightly, is not available for any discretionary decision, and is prohibited outright by other countries' laws and by most companies' own policies. And there are affirmative defences where the payment was lawful under the written law of the foreign country, or was a reasonable and bona fide expenditure such as travel directly related to the promotion of products or the performance of a contract.
The UK Bribery Act differs in ways the exam uses to contrast them.
It covers commercial bribery as well as bribery of officials — offering or receiving a bribe between private parties is an offence in its own right.
It creates four offences: bribing another person, being bribed, bribing a foreign public official, and the corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery by an associated person.
That fourth offence is the significant one. A commercial organisation is liable where someone associated with it — an employee, agent, subsidiary or other person performing services for it — bribes to obtain business for it, unless the organisation can show it had adequate procedures in place to prevent bribery. Liability is strict, subject to that defence, and the defence is the entire subject of a compliance programme.
It has no facilitation payment exception.
And it reaches any organisation carrying on business in the United Kingdom, wherever the conduct occurs.
The six principles of adequate procedures are worth learning, because they are also a complete answer to "what should a company do about bribery risk": proportionate procedures, matched to the risk and the size of the organisation; top-level commitment, which is Chapter 17's tone; risk assessment, periodic and documented; due diligence on the people who perform services for the organisation; communication, including training; and monitoring and review.
What the management accountant contributes is specific. Payments cannot be made without being recorded, so the finance function is where a bribe becomes visible: in a vague description, an unusual intermediary, a payment in the wrong country, a commission out of line with the work, a cash payment, an invoice with no deliverable. Chapter 12's controls are the mechanism, and the accounting provisions of the Act make their failure an offence in itself.
