18.3  Corporate responsibility and its four levels
The outcome asks you to discuss corporate responsibility for ethical conduct, and a good answer begins by taking the question seriously rather than asserting a position.
Two classical views frame it. The narrow view holds that a company's responsibility is to its owners: to make money within the rules of the game, lawfully and without deception, and that managers spending the owners' money on social purposes are spending money that is not theirs for objectives nobody elected them to pursue. The broader view holds that a company's licence to operate comes from society, that it affects many parties besides its owners, and that it is responsible to all of them — the stakeholder reasoning of Chapter 12.
The position that answers the exam takes the useful part of both. Even on the narrow view, a company is responsible for conduct: the narrow view says within the rules, and the rules include honesty, safety and the law. And the broader view is strengthened, not weakened, by the long-run argument — a company that damages its customers, its employees, its suppliers or its community is usually destroying value rather than transferring it, which is what Book 2's long-run value maximisation actually implies.
Four specific responsibilities for ethical conduct follow, and they are what the outcome is asking for.
For its own conduct, by the people it employs, which is the subject of Chapters 12 to 17.
For conduct in its name, by agents, distributors and partners, which section 18.2 makes a legal liability and not only an ethical one.
For conduct in its supply chain, to the extent of its influence. This is the most contested of the four: a company cannot audit every tier, and the standard applied is that it should know its material risks, use its leverage where it has any, and not arrange its affairs so as to avoid knowing.
And for the effects of its products and operations on people who never agreed to them: the customer who cannot evaluate what they are buying, the community affected by the plant, the person whose data the company holds.
The four levels of social responsibility, which the outcome asks to be defined, are the classical pyramid and provide the structure for an answer.
Economic responsibility: to be profitable. It is the foundation, because a company that does not cover its costs can meet none of the other obligations, and Book 1's whole analysis is about whether it does.
Legal responsibility: to obey the law — the floor of Chapter 15.
Ethical responsibility: to do what is right, fair and expected beyond what the law requires, which is the gap Chapter 15 described.
Philanthropic responsibility: to contribute resources to the community — donations, volunteering, support for education and the arts. It is discretionary, and the order matters: philanthropy does not compensate for failures at the levels below it, and a company that underpays its suppliers and funds a scholarship has not balanced anything.
Figure F818-02 sets the four levels out.
Figure F818-02. Four levels of social responsibility
@table
Level | Obligation | Nature | Orontes example
Economic | Be profitable | Required by owners and by survival | Earn more than the cost of capital
Legal | Obey the law | Required by society | Food safety, labelling, employment law
Ethical | Do what is right beyond the law | Expected by society | Tell a customer before they find out
Philanthropic | Contribute to the community | Desired, discretionary | Support for the agricultural college
@end
