18.4  Sustainability
Two outcomes meet here: the major issues of sustainability and social responsibility, and the three conceptual spheres.
The three spheres — sometimes the three pillars, or people, planet and profit — are the structure to use.
The economic sphere: whether the activity is financially viable over the long run, for the company and for those who depend on it. An unprofitable activity is not sustainable in any sense, which is why this sphere comes first and why sustainability and the economics of Books 1 to 3 are not opposed.
The environmental sphere: whether the activity can continue without depleting or damaging the natural systems it depends on — emissions, energy, water, waste, land, biodiversity, materials.
The social sphere: the effects on people — employees, communities, customers and supply chain workers — covering safety, labour conditions, human rights, development and community impact.
The point of the three-sphere framing is that sustainability is where they overlap. An initiative that is good environmentally and loses money indefinitely will stop; one that is profitable and damages a community will be stopped; and the useful work is in finding the overlap, which is also where a management accountant is most useful, because the overlap is identified with numbers.
The major issues the outcome asks about, grouped.
Climate and emissions: greenhouse gas emissions from a company's own operations, from the energy it buys, and from its value chain — the last of which is usually the largest and the hardest to measure. Transition risk, as regulation and markets change, and physical risk, as weather patterns do; for a food company, the physical risk is direct, since Orontes's sesame comes from fields.
Resources: energy, water, raw materials and the waste produced. These have the useful property of appearing in the accounts: Orontes's energy cost is 42,000,000 × 0.03 = 1,260,000 a year, and a 15 per cent reduction is 1,260,000 × 0.15 = 189,000 of saving as well as an emissions reduction — which is the overlap of the three spheres in one line. Process waste of 1.5 per cent of cost of goods sold is 27,300,000 × 0.015 = 409,500, and waste is both an environmental issue and the cost of poor quality of Book 1.
People: safety, pay and conditions, forced and child labour in the supply chain, diversity and inclusion, community effects.
Products: nutrition and safety for a food company, packaging and its disposal, honest marketing, and what the product does to the people who use it.
Governance and reporting: how sustainability is governed, what is disclosed, to what standard, and whether it is assured. Reporting frameworks have converged considerably — the International Sustainability Standards Board's standards building on earlier frameworks, with climate disclosure modelled on the task force recommendations — and the direction is toward disclosure that is comparable, assured and located in the financial report rather than beside it.
Two issues that produce exam questions.
Materiality, and its two senses. Financial materiality asks which sustainability matters affect the company's own cash flows and value — climate risk to the sesame crop, for example. Impact materiality asks which of the company's effects on the world are significant regardless of whether they rebound on it. Some frameworks require only the first and some require both, and an answer should say which sense it is using.
And greenwashing: claims that overstate performance, that are unmeasurable, that count something the company would have done anyway, or that offset rather than reduce. It matters ethically because it is simply dishonesty about a technical subject, and it matters practically because regulators in several jurisdictions now treat it as a misleading commercial practice. The discipline is the one this book has applied throughout: state the measure, the basis, the period and the comparison, and do not claim what cannot be verified.
