LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Whistleblower", "stakeholder" and "sustainable"
Three words that carry arguments inside them. Whistleblower is an unfortunate English coinage: it suggests an outsider interrupting a game, and in many organisations it has acquired the sense of informer, which is why codes increasingly use speak up or raise a concern instead — the act is ordinary and the vocabulary should be too. Arabic الإبلاغ عن المخالفات, reporting violations, is neutral and better; French lanceur d'alerte, one who raises an alarm, is better still, since an alarm is something one is grateful for. Stakeholder was coined as a deliberate play on shareholder, and the play is the argument: it asserts that others hold a stake too. And sustainable has been used so widely that it now means little on its own — a sustainable product, a sustainable margin, a sustainable pace. In a report, replace it with what is meant: emissions reduced by a stated amount against a stated baseline, or water use per tonne, or a margin that does not depend on deferring maintenance. The general word is where greenwashing lives; the specific one cannot be greenwashed.
