Chapter summary
Figure F818-04 brings the chapter together.
Figure F818-04. Chapter 18 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A whistleblowing framework is how wrongdoing is actually found, since tips outrank every other detection method; it makes the code operative, extends the resolution ladder when that ladder is blocked, gives early warning and changes behaviour before it is used — requiring several routes including one outside the management line, confidentiality with anonymity available, explicit and enforced protection from retaliation, accessibility to everybody including agents, a defined process with feedback, reporting to the audit committee, and above all a demonstrated history. Anti-bribery laws exist to protect public decisions, fair competition and the integrity of records: the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act prohibits anything of value to a foreign official to obtain or retain business, including through intermediaries, and separately requires accurate books and records and internal accounting control, with a narrow facilitation exception and defences for conduct lawful under written local law or for reasonable promotional expenditure; the UK Bribery Act covers commercial bribery, creates four offences including the corporate failure to prevent bribery by an associated person, allows only the defence of adequate procedures under six principles, has no facilitation exception, and reaches any organisation doing business in the United Kingdom. Corporate responsibility for conduct covers the company's own people, conduct in its name by agents, its supply chain to the extent of its influence, and the effects of its products — structured by the four levels of economic, legal, ethical and philanthropic responsibility, in that order, so that philanthropy compensates for nothing below it. Sustainability has three spheres — economic, environmental and social — and lives where they overlap, as a 15 per cent cut in 42,000,000 × 0.03 = 1,260,000 of energy cost shows at 1,260,000 × 0.15 = 189,000, with major issues in climate and emissions, resources and waste at 27,300,000 × 0.015 = 409,500, people, products, and governance and reporting, and with materiality in two senses and greenwashing as the characteristic failure. And data ethics applies fairness tested in outcomes, privacy beyond nominal consent, transparency about decisions, ownership that rests with the person, and accountability that no model can carry — supported by data protection regulation whose purpose is to protect individuals' rights through lawful bases, purpose limitation and minimisation, accuracy and retention limits, security and breach notification, individual rights including over automated decisions, demonstrable accountability, cross-border restrictions and penalties measured against turnover.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P18-01  Occupational fraud is most often detected by:
A.	tips.
B.	external audit.
C.	internal audit.
D.	accident.
