A.	at least one route outside the management line.
B.	named reporting only.
C.	investigation by the subject's manager.
D.	a single annual report.
P18-03  Protection from retaliation must extend to:
A.	demotion, exclusion and reassignment as well as dismissal.
B.	dismissal only.
C.	employees only.
D.	proven concerns only.
P18-04  The accounting provisions of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act concern:
A.	books, records and internal accounting control.
B.	political donations.
C.	audit fees.
D.	tax filings.
P18-05  The UK Bribery Act's corporate offence is:
A.	failing to prevent bribery by an associated person.
B.	paying a foreign official.
C.	failing to report bribery.
D.	inaccurate accounting.
P18-06  "Adequate procedures" rest on six principles beginning with:
A.	proportionate procedures and top-level commitment.
B.	insurance and indemnity.
C.	audit and prosecution.
D.	disclosure and consent.
P18-07  Which level of social responsibility is discretionary?
A.	Philanthropic
B.	Economic
C.	Legal
D.	Ethical
P18-08  The three spheres of sustainability are:
A.	economic, environmental, social.
B.	economic, legal, ethical.
C.	climate, water, waste.
D.	profit, people, philanthropy.
P18-09  Energy cost of 3 per cent of 42,000,000 of sales is:
A.	1,260,000
B.	126,000
C.	12,600,000
D.	420,000
P18-10  Waste of 1.5 per cent of cost of goods sold of 27,300,000 is:
A.	409,500
B.	40,950
C.	4,095,000
D.	273,000
P18-11  Greenwashing is:
A.	a claim that overstates environmental performance or cannot be verified.
B.	a reporting standard.
C.	an assurance procedure.
D.	a carbon market.
P18-12  Accountability in data ethics means:
A.	a person or company is answerable, since a model cannot be.
B.	the model is responsible for its outputs.
C.	the user accepts the risk.
D.	the regulator decides each case.
Case-style practice: three items for the audit committee
Orontes's audit committee has three items. First, the whistleblowing line received one call in two years and the committee is asked to approve its renewal at 12,000 a year. Second, the company is about to appoint an agent in a new market who will be paid 4 per cent of sales, and the agent is a former official of the ministry that licenses food imports there. Third, marketing proposes to buy a list of 40,000 consumer records from a distributor, and the proposal says the distributor "confirms the data is compliant".
Required. Advise the committee on each item. Under 400 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC18-1  Answer A. Tips, chiefly from employees.
B is wrong. External audit detects a small proportion.
C is wrong. Internal audit detects more than external but less than tips.
D is wrong. Review detects less than tips.
SC18-2  Answer A. The highest-risk concerns go unreported.
B is wrong. It is less effective.
C is wrong. It is incomplete.
D is wrong. Malice is addressed by investigation, not by naming.
SC18-3  Answer A. The precedent set by the first case.
B is wrong. Wording persuades nobody.
C is wrong. Training explains the route only.
D is wrong. The provider is a mechanism.
SC18-4  Answer A. Accurate records and internal accounting control.
B is wrong. No general disclosure is required.
C is wrong. Regulators do not approve agents.
D is wrong. Hotlines come from other rules.
SC18-5  Answer A. Failure to prevent by an associated person.
B is wrong. That is a separate offence.
C is wrong. So is that.
D is wrong. That belongs to the US accounting provisions.
SC18-6  Answer A. Adequate procedures is the statutory defence.
B is wrong. Knowledge is not required for the offence.
C is wrong. Custom is no defence.
D is wrong. Size does not matter.
SC18-7  Answer A. Philanthropic completes the four.
B is wrong. Environmental is a sustainability sphere.
C is wrong. Regulatory is part of legal.
D is wrong. Fiduciary is a different duty.
SC18-8  Answer A. Without viability nothing else is possible.
B is wrong. It is the foundation, not the sole objective.
C is wrong. Law does not require profit.
D is wrong. Shareholder demand is not the reason given.
SC18-9  Answer A. Philanthropy does not offset lower levels.
B is wrong. The ethical level was failed.
C is wrong. Obligations below were not met.
D is wrong. The economic level is about viability.
SC18-10  Answer A. Economic, environmental, social.
B is wrong. Those are levels of responsibility.
C is wrong. Philanthropy is not a sphere.
D is wrong. Those are environmental issues.
SC18-11  Answer A. 1,260,000 × 0.15 = 189,000.
B is wrong. That is ten times too small.
C is wrong. That is the remaining cost.
D is wrong. That is the whole cost.
SC18-12  Answer A. Effects on the world.
B is wrong. That is financial materiality.
C is wrong. Legal requirement is separate.
D is wrong. Insurability is unrelated.
SC18-13  Answer A. Outcomes must be tested.
B is wrong. Intention does not prevent disparate outcomes.
C is wrong. Consent concerns privacy.
D is wrong. A model cannot hold a duty.
SC18-14  Answer A. It is not meaningful agreement.
B is wrong. Legality is a floor, not the standard.
C is wrong. Accountability cannot be transferred this way.
D is wrong. Regulation requires a valid basis.
SC18-15  Answer A. Protecting individuals' rights.
B is wrong. Companies bear the obligations.
C is wrong. Standardisation is not the purpose.
D is wrong. Competition is a different field.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
How most fraud is detected | Tips, chiefly from employees — more than audit and review combined.
The UK corporate offence and its defence | Failing to prevent bribery by an associated person; the defence is adequate procedures.
The four levels of responsibility | Economic, legal, ethical and philanthropic.
The three spheres of sustainability | Economic, environmental and social.
Saving from a 15 per cent energy cut | 189,000: 1,260,000 × 0.15 = 189,000.
The five principles of data ethics | Fairness, privacy, transparency, ownership and accountability.
What it tells you | Five lists and one calculation — and the calculation is there to show that the sustainability sphere where the three overlap is found with figures, which is the management accountant's part of all of this.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P18-01  Answer A. Tips lead every other method.
B is wrong. External audit is a small proportion.
C is wrong. Internal audit detects less than tips.
D is wrong. Accident is a residual category.
P18-02  Answer A. A route outside management.
B is wrong. Anonymity must be available.
C is wrong. The subject's manager is not independent.
D is wrong. Annual reporting is not the mechanism.
P18-03  Answer A. All forms of detriment.
B is wrong. Dismissal is one form.
C is wrong. Agents and contractors should be covered.
D is wrong. Good-faith reports are protected regardless.
P18-04  Answer A. Records and internal accounting control.
B is wrong. Donations are a separate topic.
C is wrong. Fees are unrelated.
D is wrong. Tax is a different regime.
P18-05  Answer A. The failure-to-prevent offence.
B is wrong. That is a separate offence.
C is wrong. No general failure-to-report offence exists there.
D is wrong. That is the US accounting provision.
P18-06  Answer A. Proportionality and top-level commitment.
B is wrong. Insurance is not a principle.
C is wrong. Prosecution is an outcome.
D is wrong. Consent belongs to data protection.
P18-07  Answer A. Philanthropy is discretionary.
B is wrong. Economic is required.
C is wrong. Legal is required.
D is wrong. Ethical is expected.
P18-08  Answer A. Economic, environmental, social.
B is wrong. Those are responsibility levels.
C is wrong. Those are issues.
D is wrong. Philanthropy is not a sphere.
P18-09  Answer A. 42,000,000 × 0.03 = 1,260,000.
B is wrong. That is ten times too small.
C is wrong. That is ten times too large.
D is wrong. That is 1 per cent.
P18-10  Answer A. 27,300,000 × 0.015 = 409,500.
B is wrong. That is ten times too small.
C is wrong. That is ten times too large.
D is wrong. That is 1 per cent.
P18-11  Answer A. Overstated or unverifiable claims.
B is wrong. It is not a standard.
C is wrong. Assurance is the opposite.
D is wrong. A market is a mechanism.
P18-12  Answer A. Somebody must be answerable.
B is wrong. A model cannot bear responsibility.
C is wrong. Risk does not transfer to the user.
D is wrong. Regulators adjudicate; they do not hold the duty.
Case-style set: three items for the audit committee
The whistleblowing line. One call in two years from four hundred employees is not evidence that the line is unnecessary; it is evidence that it is not trusted or not known, and cancelling it would remove the mechanism that detects more wrongdoing than audit and management review combined. I would renew it and fix it: add a route to the committee chair directly; confirm anonymity is available; publish, with the renewal, what happened to the one concern that was raised; state the protection from retaliation in terms that cover exclusion and reassignment, not only dismissal; make it available to agents, contractors and suppliers in their own languages; and ask the committee to receive a standing report of concerns raised, categories, time taken and outcomes. The 12,000 is not the decision; whether people believe the line works is the decision.
The agent. This is the highest-risk item on the agenda. A 4 per cent commission is not in itself improper, but an agent who is a former official of the ministry that licenses the company's imports is the exact profile the anti-bribery laws are written about, and the company would be liable under the UK Bribery Act's failure-to-prevent offence for a bribe paid by that agent to obtain business, with adequate procedures its only defence. Before any appointment I would require documented due diligence covering the agent's ownership, continuing relationships with the ministry and reputation; a written contract with a defined scope, deliverables, anti-bribery warranties, audit rights and termination on breach; payment by bank transfer in the country of performance against invoices, never in cash or to a third country; a commission benchmarked to what the services are worth; and training and periodic certification. If the due diligence cannot be completed, the appointment should not proceed — and the committee should note that the finance function's own records are where any problem will first be visible.
The consumer list. "The distributor confirms the data is compliant" is an assurance, not evidence, and the liability and the reputation would be Orontes's. I would require written evidence of the lawful basis on which the 40,000 records were collected and of what those people were told, including whether sharing with another company was disclosed; a contract with warranties and an indemnity; minimisation of what is taken and deletion after the campaign; a clear opt-out in the first contact; and a documented assessment. The test that decides it is simpler than any of that: would these people recognise this use as something they agreed to? If not, the campaign should not run, whatever the distributor confirms.
